<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ossetians</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ossetians are an Iranian-speaking people of the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, living in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-ossetia-alania">North Ossetia–Alania</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-ossetia">South Ossetia</a>. They descend from the medieval Alans, who withdrew into the mountains after the Mongol and Timurid invasions. Most are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> Christians, and their language is the main surviving descendant of the Scytho-Sarmatian languages.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ossetians.jpg" alt="Ossetians" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nardo Polo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_North_Ossetia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Ossetians, also called Ossetes or Ossets, are an Iranian people indigenous to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ossetia">Ossetia</a>, a region on the northern and southern sides of the Caucasus Mountains. They speak Ossetian, an Eastern Iranian language and a remnant of the Scytho-Sarmatian dialect group once spoken across the Pontic–Caspian Steppe; it is not mutually intelligible with any other Iranian language. The names Ossetians and Ossetia come from the Russian Osetin, borrowed from the Georgian Oseti, 'the land of the Osi', a term used since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> for the Iranian-speaking population of the central Caucasus. It derives ultimately from As, the self-designation of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alanic-language">Alanic</a> tribe of the Sarmatians.</p><p>They descend from the Alans. Between 350 and 374 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> destroyed the Alan kingdom between the Don and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>; some Alans fled west and took part in the invasions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, while others went south and settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, where they established the medieval kingdom of Alania.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From the 8th century a consolidated Alania existed in the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, a centralised monarchy with a strong military force and an economy that benefited from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>. It reached its peak in the 11th century under the ruler Durgulel, who established relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> first met the Alans in 1222, and a second campaign in 1238 and 1239 captured much of the Alan plain, after which Alania ceased to exist as a political entity. The Alans withdrew into the mountains, where they formed three groups, the Iron, the Digoron and the Kudar.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ossetia">Ossetia</a> became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> in 1774. In 1922 the South Ossetian autonomous oblast was created within the Georgian SSR, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-ossetia-alania">North Ossetia</a> remained in the Russian SFSR, and in 1994 North Ossetia added the name Alania to its official title.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Before the 10th century the Ossetians were pagan; Byzantine missionaries partly converted them early in the 10th century, and by the 13th century most of the urban population had become Eastern Orthodox through Georgian missionary work. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> spread among the Digor in the 1500s and 1600s through contact with the Kabarday Circassians. Most Ossetians are Eastern Orthodox Christians, while many follow the folk religion Uatsdin, also called Assianism, which has temples known as kuvandon and ritual animal sacrifices. The Narts, a cycle of mythology, form the basis of Ossetian folk literature.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ossetians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Ossetians</b> of the central Caucasus speak a language descended from Alanic and preserve the Nart epic tradition, which shares motifs with older steppe lore.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alans">Alans · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…Sarmatian culture deepest into later history: one branch followed the Vandals into Gaul, Spain and North Africa, where the Vandal kings styled themselves kings of the Vandals and Alans, while another remained in the Caucasus, where their descendants, the <b>Ossetians</b>, still speak an Iranian language derived from Alanic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sarmatians">Sarmatians · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Survivors withdrew into the mountains, where their descendants became the <b>Ossetians</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-alania">Kingdom of Alania</a></li><li><blockquote>After the February Revolution of 1917, a congress at Vladikavkaz founded the Union of United Mountaineers of the North Caucasus and Dagestan, bringing together Chechens, Ingush, <b>Ossetians</b>, Kabardians, Dagestanis and others.</blockquote><a href="/state/mountainous-republic-of-the-northern-caucasus">Mountainous Republic of the Northern Caucasus</a></li><li><blockquote>Its princes extended their power over neighbouring <b>Ossetians</b>, Ingush, Balkars and Abazins.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-kabarda">Principality of Kabarda</a></li><li><blockquote>It covered the lands of the Terek Cossack Host and of the Chechens, Ingush, <b>Ossetians</b> and Kabardians, with its government at Pyatigorsk and Vladikavkaz.</blockquote><a href="/state/terek-soviet-republic">Terek Soviet Republic</a></li></ul></section>
